`Statement::Solve(Box<SolveStatement>)` uses outer statement tag 25; old outer tags 0 through 24 retain their meanings. No variant asserts generic radical impossibility in this version. Exact mathematical emptiness is Coverage of a set with Empty branches, not Outcome::Refused and not an empty candidate list. General zero polynomial solving returns the whole solution domain by an identity family; a nonzero constant polynomial returns a checked empty set. Zero-polynomial multiplicity remains unsupported, as in the existing square-free service.

**Definition (Outcome integration)** · `def:nrz:cas-solving-design-outcomes`

Add `Outcome::Solved { request, answer: SolveAnswer, usage }` and `Outcome::SolvePartial { request, answer: SolveAnswer, reason: SolveReason, usage }`. Solved proves the requested goal and presentation on the requested region. Complete piece claims have no missing roots there even while a requested whole-domain answer is partial for lack of a separate cover. Radicals requires actual radical provenance; AnyExact accepts RealExact with Real radicals or count-certified cells. RequestedRealRadicalsUnavailable preserves completed Real coverage at AnyExact strength. The zero-polynomial identity family is an admissible radical-free presentation with its bound arbitrary coordinate. All hypotheses and guarded conditions remain visible (`dec:nrz:cas-symbol-sorts-real-contract`).

Extend existing Unresolved with `solve_reason: Option<SolveReason>` and existing Exhausted with `solve_best: Option<Box<SolveAnswer>>`; other services set these to None and retain existing result fields. A solve with no checked fact is Unresolved. Exhausted always reports the first failing BudgetKind, requested amount, limit and location; arithmetic range and checked capacity overflow remain resource faults. Only previously independently replayed facts survive. An exhausted coefficient branch never creates a whole-region cover. Invalid reports bad handles, scopes, schema, wire or request coverage; Refused retains mathematical domain/ordering refusals with evidence; extend RefusalReason with ComplexOrdering, IncompatibleSelection and RefutedBranch, while SolveReason identifies a missing proof rather than an established contradiction. Unsupported remains available for fields such as positive characteristic outside this executable fragment.

SolveReason remains a closed enum with precise coefficient, polynomial, unit, selection, coverage, presentation, isolation, ordering, regularity and neighborhood boundaries. Add RealProblemHasComplexSymbol, SpacePlacementUnavailable, AmbientSpaceAmbiguous, PieceMembershipPending, BaseCoveragePending, FibreCoveragePending and RequestedRealRadicalsUnavailable as specified in (`alg:nrz:cas-symbol-sorts-routing`); structural contradictions instead use SymbolSortMismatch or AmbientSpaceMismatch. NoApplicablePiece is a witnessed outside-support refusal, not an invalid declaration. Bounded blockers retain relevant objects, region and obligation. A generic independent degree n ≥ 5 retains GenericRadicalRouteUnavailable, while admitted binomials still use their product proof; exhaustion proves no impossibility.

## Fallible coefficient capability · `sec:nrz:cas-solving-design-coefficients`

**Definition (One arithmetic interface, explicit algebra capability)** · `def:nrz:cas-solving-design-coefficient-trait`

The crate-private sealed trait `ExactCoefficients` has associated `Element` and the methods `domain() -> Handle`, `zero(&mut Budget)`, `one(&mut Budget)`, `copy(&Element, &mut Budget)`, `neg(&Element, &mut Budget)`, `add(&Element, &Element, &mut Budget)`, `sub(&Element, &Element, &mut Budget)`, `mul(&Element, &Element, &mut Budget)`, `pow(&Element, u32, &mut Budget)`, `reduce(&Element, &mut Budget)`, `zero_test(&Element, &EvaluationScope, &mut Budget)`, `inverse(&Element, &EvaluationScope, &mut Budget)` and `map(&Element, &CoefficientMap, &EvaluationScope, &mut Budget)`. Ordinary element operations return `Result<Element, CoefficientFailure>`; zero_test returns `Result<ZeroDecision, CoefficientFailure>` and inverse returns `Result<UnitDecision<Element>, CoefficientFailure>`. EvaluationScope contains full context, coefficient Region, available sealed premises and the finite localization domain. No public `Add`, `Mul`, `Div`, `Field` or `Num` implementation can bypass this interface for budgeted values.

`ZeroDecision` is Zero(CoefficientCertificate), Nonzero(CoefficientCertificate), Split(SplitCertificate) or Undetermined(GuardedObligation). `UnitDecision` is Unit { inverse, certificate }, Zero(certificate), Split(certificate) or Undetermined(obligation). Certificates returned by arithmetic are untrusted until replay; no arithmetic method constructs VerifiedWitness. `CoefficientFailure` is Fault for invalid/range/exhaustion, or Unsupported(domain feature). Mathematical zero and a required component split are ordinary decisions, not resource errors. Polynomial algorithms depend on these decisions, never on `!= zero()` as a field-unit test. Division requires Unit; it does not assume that every nonzero vector in a quotient is invertible.

The implementations are RationalCoefficients, GaussianCoefficients, FractionCoefficients and TowerCoefficients. Fraction values use coprime multivariate numerator/denominator over the admitted base, with denominator leading coefficient one and decreasing graded-lex terms. Canonicalization uses recursive primitive-part/subresultant gcd in the fixed variable order; a claimed gcd is checked by exact divisibilities and recursive content/primitive-part coprimality evidence, with univariate Bezout over the fraction field of the preceding variables. Coprime multivariate polynomials need not generate the unit ideal, so no multivariate Bezout-to-one premise is assumed. All recursive coefficient work is charged. If this route is outside budget, return Exhausted rather than retaining a noncanonical value. A denominator may be nonzero in the formal fraction field while lacking nonvanishing evidence on the evaluation region; such a value has a definedness guard. A valid partial specialization need only act on the finite localization generated by denominators actually used, and its key includes that set.

Tower elements are reduced vectors over their parent, with length below the defining degree; multiplication is schoolbook convolution followed by monic reduction. For an inverse of h modulo m, compute a monic gcd g and Bezout coefficients. If g = 1, replay hA + mB = 1 and reduce A. If g = m, the element is zero on that level. Otherwise stage a ComponentSplit with g and m/g, prove their product and coprimality using square-freeness of m, restrict all dependent data and continue on both children. If a parent coefficient inversion requires a split, split the parent first and rebuild higher levels under both restriction maps; never use a child computation to justify an earlier parent inversion. Strict decrease of the split degree bounds the component tree, while the configured limits may stop it earlier.

On a parameter locus where a defining polynomial loses degree or square-freeness, normalize and partition that locus before constructing a reduced tower. A repeated polynomial is handled through its square-free support plus separate multiplicity evidence; nilpotents are not smuggled into a reduced algebra. Square-freeness is certified by a checked Unit for the formal derivative modulo the defining monic polynomial; this supplies the square-free premise to extension.split even when the old rational-only SquareFree statement does not apply. Reducedness is checked inductively from Q/Q(i), rational-function domains and these derivative-unit certificates. Root existence in C and arithmetic in a finite quotient are distinct: a selected complex root supplies a map from the quotient to C, and product completeness may use C's field theorem after that map is checked even if the quotient was not irreducible. A general irreducibility assertion still needs its own certificate; square-free is insufficient. This release admits Fraction bases Q and Q(i), and canonical flattenings/renamings of those rational-function fields. It does not construct a fraction field over an unproved quotient. Executable injective maps are identity, Q into Q(i), the canonical Q/Q(i) constants into C, independent-variable renaming/inclusion, and the canonical parent inclusion into a monic quotient. Canonical constant/parent inclusions have an empty image vector and are uniquely determined by their source/target; independent-variable maps carry ordered images. The standard Gaussian constant embedding fixes the meaning of i without referring back to the ExactCoefficient expression; all other claimed Embedding maps remain unavailable until a checker proves injectivity. Root evaluation and component projection use Specialization, with the finite localization obligations retained.

**Table (Arithmetic preflight and metering)** · `tab:nrz:cas-solving-design-arithmetic-bounds`

|Operation|Reservation before allocation or dependency call|
|---------|------------------------------------------------|
|Integer copy, sign, compare, add/subtract|For maximum operand bits B, reserve at least L(B) = max(1, ceil(B/64)) limb work; sum bits at most B + 1. Copy charges retained bytes as well.|
|Integer multiply or quotient/remainder|Product bits at most B₁ + B₂, quotient/remainder bits at most the dividend bound; reserve C(B) = 8(L(B) + 1)² at B equal to the maximum operand/intermediate bound. Zero divisor is rejected first.|
|Integer gcd and exact quotient|Use the bounded Euclidean quotient/remainder route with at most 2B + 1 remainders for B-bit nonnegative operands; reserve each call before execution. A bulk num-integer helper is allowed only after reserving this complete envelope and verifying its implementation fits that envelope; otherwise the metered route using Integer::div_rem is mandatory.|
|Rational add/subtract|Input components at most B bits give raw numerator at most 2B + 1 and denominator at most 2B. Prepay products, addition, reduction gcd and two exact quotients; check reduced components. Construct canonical Ratio with new_raw only after independently established gcd/positive-denominator invariants.|
|Rational multiply/divide|Preflight raw component bits at most 2B, cross-cancellation gcds and exact quotients, then products; division first checks nonzero numerator. No Ratio operator with hidden unchecked work is called.|
|Rational operation accounting envelope|For B-bit admitted inputs, any one add/subtract/multiply/divide above costs at most R(B) = (8B + 32) C(2B + 1) arithmetic_steps, including copies, comparisons, gcd iterations and final checks; measured smaller routes may charge less, never more without another preflight.|
|Gaussian add/multiply/inverse|At most 2 rational additions; 4 products plus 2 additions; or norm a² + b² followed by two divisions and one negation. Reserve the complete sequence, with intermediate component bounds propagated after each scalar step. Conjugation and components copy with charge.|
|Sparse polynomial add/multiply|Reserve m + n output slots for addition; mn term products and at most mn accumulator insertions for multiplication. Exponent addition is checked; charge every ordered-key comparison by variable count and every coefficient operation recursively. Normalization sorts with a deterministic merge sort bound of N ceil(log₂ max(2,N)) key comparisons.|
|Fraction add/multiply/inverse|Cross products reserve the polynomial bounds above, then primitive-part/gcd normalization with every recursive division charged; inverse swaps normalized numerator/denominator only after a regional unit decision. No polynomial term count is estimated from the final cancellation alone.|
|Tower multiply/reduce at degree d|Reserve at most d² parent products and d² parent additions for convolution; at most (d − 1)d parent products and additions for descending monic reduction; charge scans, copies and coefficient tests. At each level propagate actual parent budgets.|
|Tower gcd/Bezout at degree d|At most d + 1 Euclidean divisions per component, with each long-division cancellation charged and each leading inverse returning Unit/Split/Undetermined; reserve coefficient and degree bounds before each step, not an unbounded whole gcd call.|
|Power and perfect-power test|Repeated squaring uses at most 2 floor(log₂ e) + 1 multiplications for positive exponent e. For B-bit magnitude and positive order n, perfect-power testing uses at most ceil(B/n) + 1 binary-search trials, each at most 2 floor(log₂ n) + 1 multiplications with a conservative B + 2n intermediate-bit bound, followed by exact exponentiation to verify the answer; a floor root alone is no certificate. Negative/even-root cases have explicit field/realness conditions.|

All predicted additions and products of sizes use checked u64 arithmetic and checked usize/u32 conversions. Overflow is a named capacity exhaustion before allocation. Existing component_bits and intermediate_bits bound every bigint, including temporary normalized-denominator work. `coefficient_ops` counts every invocation of the sealed trait, including zero tests, copies and recursively invoked parent operations; arithmetic_steps and work additionally charge scalar limb work. `gcd_steps`, `tower_reductions` and the other counters below are monotone nested counters, not replacements for work. Cache hits charge key comparison/copy costs and cannot erase construction or replay work. Failed transactions keep usage but publish neither values nor evidence.

